<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<config xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="http://qconsulting.ca/fo3/ModConfig5.0.xsd">

  <!-- ⚠ ELEMENT ORDER IS FIXED BY THE SCHEMA and Vortex enforces it strictly (MO2 does not):
       moduleName -> moduleImage -> moduleDependencies -> requiredInstallFiles -> installSteps
                  -> conditionalFileInstalls
       Putting requiredInstallFiles after installSteps parses fine as XML but fails Vortex with
       "Sch_InvalidElementContentExpecting". -->

  <moduleName>Precision Physic Bodies</moduleName>

  <!-- Banner shown at the top of every installer page.
       Path is relative to the ARCHIVE ROOT, backslashes, and the file must actually
       exist or Vortex/MO2 will error on install. -->
  <moduleImage path="fomod\images\header.jpg" showImage="true" showFade="true" height="-1"/>

  <!-- Core payload: always installed, nothing to choose.
       All four race skeletons ship together on purpose — the race map points a race at a
       skeleton file, and a map entry pointing at a file that was not installed would leave
       those NPCs without a skeleton. They are ~100 KB each; which races are actually driven
       is decided by PPB_Skeletons_Added_Race.ini, which the user can edit at any time. -->
  <requiredInstallFiles>
    <folder source="00 Core" destination="" priority="0"/>
  </requiredInstallFiles>

  <installSteps order="Explicit">

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Performance: SMP hair & tail rigs on/off.
         The rigs are gated by ONE knob (npcFollower) in PPB_tuning.txt, so the
         "off" option installs a tuning file that differs from the core one by
         exactly that line. The variant is GENERATED from the canonical file at
         package time (package_release.py) — never hand-edited — so the two can
         not drift apart.
         ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <installStep name="Performance">
      <optionalFileGroups order="Explicit">

        <group name="SMP hair and tail collision" type="SelectExactlyOne">
          <plugins order="Explicit">

            <plugin name="On - full experience (recommended)">
              <description>
Hair and tails get collision capsules and react to your hands and weapons — on both
Faster HDT-SMP and HDT-SMP Flex.

Cost is bounded: only the 2 NPCs nearest you (within ~10 m) carry live rigs, and a
closer NPC takes the slot from a farther one. A dense wig is 85-200 physics bodies,
which is exactly why that budget exists.

You can tune it later in PPB_tuning.txt: npcRigMaxActors (how many NPCs), npcRigRangeU
(the range), npcFollower 0 (kill switch, takes effect within a second, no restart).
              </description>
              <files/>
              <typeDescriptor><type name="Recommended"/></typeDescriptor>
            </plugin>

            <plugin name="Off - performance">
              <description>
No hair or tail rigs at all: NPC hair and tails will NOT react to your touch, and your
hand passes through them. Everything else in PPB is unaffected (body capsules, joints,
touch detection on the body, heel fix).

Pick this on a machine that struggles in crowds. It installs the same PPB_tuning.txt
with one line changed: npcFollower 0.

You can change your mind any time by editing that line back to 1 — no reinstall needed,
it takes effect within a second, even mid-game.
              </description>
              <files>
                <folder source="20 Performance - no SMP garment rigs" destination="" priority="1"/>
              </files>
              <typeDescriptor><type name="Optional"/></typeDescriptor>
            </plugin>

          </plugins>
        </group>

      </optionalFileGroups>
    </installStep>

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Optional features (2026-09-11; FLAGS 2026-09-12; six switches in 2.2.0).
         The switches live in PPB.ini [Features]; each is ANDed with its tuning
         master, so a user can still flip them by hand later without reinstalling.

         WHY FLAGS: all six share ONE file. Plain options that each install a
         PPB.ini would clobber each other (the last one written wins). So every
         choice here only sets a FLAG and installs nothing; the single matching
         PPB.ini variant is picked in the conditional-install block at the bottom.
         The variants AND that block are GENERATED by package_release.py from one
         table, each variant verified to differ from the core file by exactly the
         intended keys.

         The four push outcomes are SUB-features of push/shove, so their page is
         only shown when push is On; with push Off they are moot and no pattern
         reads their flags.
         ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <installStep name="Optional Features">
      <optionalFileGroups order="Explicit">

        <group name="Push and shove" type="SelectExactlyOne">
          <plugins order="Explicit">
            <plugin name="On (recommended)">
              <description>
NPCs react physically when you push them with your hands or a weapon.

Choosing On opens a page where you pick which reactions you want, one by one:
  - Push walk: a steady press makes the NPC step back.
  - Push stumble: a firm push makes the NPC stagger.
  - Shove knockdown: a hard shove knocks the NPC to the ground.
  - Leg sweep knockdown: sweeping the NPC's feet knocks them down.

Busy NPCs are always left alone: anyone sitting, sleeping, working at a crafting
station or doing a chore, fighting, or in a kill move. NPCs leaning on a wall, rail
or counter can't be pushed (they are braced), but their legs can still be swept.
              </description>
              <conditionFlags>
                <flag name="ppbPush">On</flag>
              </conditionFlags>
              <typeDescriptor><type name="Recommended"/></typeDescriptor>
            </plugin>
            <plugin name="Off">
              <description>
Your hands and weapons never push, stagger or knock down an NPC, and sweeping
their legs does nothing. All four push reactions are off, whatever the next page
would have said.

The rest of PPB works as usual: body collision, touch sensing, the heel fix, and
equip gestures if you keep them. You can change this later with bPushShove in
SKSE/Plugins/PPB.ini. The change applies within a second, even mid-game.
              </description>
              <conditionFlags>
                <flag name="ppbPush">Off</flag>
              </conditionFlags>
              <typeDescriptor><type name="Optional"/></typeDescriptor>
            </plugin>
          </plugins>
        </group>

        <group name="Equip gestures" type="SelectExactlyOne">
          <plugins order="Explicit">
            <plugin name="On (recommended)">
              <description>
Dress and undress NPCs with your hands:
  - Equip: hold a piece of clothing or armour against the matching part of the
    NPC's body for about a second, then let go. It goes on them, and it stays on
    (their outfit won't swap it back).
  - Undress: grab something the NPC is wearing with both hands and pull it off.

If that slot is already taken, the item falls instead of silently replacing what
they wear. No messages pop up on screen.
              </description>
              <conditionFlags>
                <flag name="ppbEquip">On</flag>
              </conditionFlags>
              <typeDescriptor><type name="Recommended"/></typeDescriptor>
            </plugin>
            <plugin name="Off">
              <description>
Holding clothing or armour against an NPC won't equip it, and pulling with both
hands won't undress them. Pick this if another mod handles equipping by hand for
you (for example Gift by Hand).

You can change this later with bEquipGestures in SKSE/Plugins/PPB.ini. This one
is read when the game loads, so it needs a restart.
              </description>
              <conditionFlags>
                <flag name="ppbEquip">Off</flag>
              </conditionFlags>
              <typeDescriptor><type name="Optional"/></typeDescriptor>
            </plugin>
          </plugins>
        </group>

      </optionalFileGroups>
    </installStep>

    <!-- 2.2.0: the four push outcomes as separate choices (user, 2026-09-12). Each maps to one PPB.ini key and is hot. -->
    <installStep name="Push and Shove Options">
      <visible>
        <flagDependency flag="ppbPush" value="On"/>
      </visible>
      <optionalFileGroups order="Explicit">

        <group name="Push walk" type="SelectExactlyOne">
          <plugins order="Explicit">
            <plugin name="On (recommended)">
              <description>
Press your hand or weapon steadily against an NPC and they step back, away from
your hand, for as long as you keep pressing. They stop when you stop.
This is the gentle end of pushing: moving someone out of your way.

PPB.ini key: bPushWalk. Applies within a second, even mid-game.
              </description>
              <conditionFlags>
                <flag name="ppbWalk">On</flag>
              </conditionFlags>
              <typeDescriptor><type name="Recommended"/></typeDescriptor>
            </plugin>
            <plugin name="Off">
              <description>
A steady press doesn't move the NPC. Your hand still collides with them and
touch is still sensed, and the stumble, shove knockdown and leg sweep work as
you choose below.

PPB.ini key: bPushWalk.
              </description>
              <conditionFlags>
                <flag name="ppbWalk">Off</flag>
              </conditionFlags>
              <typeDescriptor><type name="Optional"/></typeDescriptor>
            </plugin>
          </plugins>
        </group>

        <group name="Push stumble" type="SelectExactlyOne">
          <plugins order="Explicit">
            <plugin name="On (recommended)">
              <description>
Push an NPC firmly enough to bend them backwards and they stumble: the game's
own stagger animation, turned to match the direction you pushed from.

If you turn Shove knockdown off but keep this on, a hard shove also makes them
stumble instead of falling.

PPB.ini key: bPushStumble. Applies within a second, even mid-game.
              </description>
              <conditionFlags>
                <flag name="ppbStumble">On</flag>
              </conditionFlags>
              <typeDescriptor><type name="Recommended"/></typeDescriptor>
            </plugin>
            <plugin name="Off">
              <description>
A firm push never makes an NPC stagger. The walk, shove knockdown and leg sweep
are not affected.

PPB.ini key: bPushStumble.
              </description>
              <conditionFlags>
                <flag name="ppbStumble">Off</flag>
              </conditionFlags>
              <typeDescriptor><type name="Optional"/></typeDescriptor>
            </plugin>
          </plugins>
        </group>

        <group name="Shove knockdown (ragdoll)" type="SelectExactlyOne">
          <plugins order="Explicit">
            <plugin name="On (recommended)">
              <description>
Shove an NPC hard and they're knocked off their feet: they go limp (ragdoll),
fall, and get back up on their own.

PPB.ini key: bShoveRagdoll. Applies within a second, even mid-game.
              </description>
              <conditionFlags>
                <flag name="ppbRagdoll">On</flag>
              </conditionFlags>
              <typeDescriptor><type name="Recommended"/></typeDescriptor>
            </plugin>
            <plugin name="Off">
              <description>
A shove never knocks an NPC down. With Push stumble on, a hard shove makes them
stumble instead. With both off, a hard shove does nothing.

This does NOT turn off the leg sweep. That is its own choice, below.

PPB.ini key: bShoveRagdoll.
              </description>
              <conditionFlags>
                <flag name="ppbRagdoll">Off</flag>
              </conditionFlags>
              <typeDescriptor><type name="Optional"/></typeDescriptor>
            </plugin>
          </plugins>
        </group>

        <group name="Leg sweep knockdown (ragdoll)" type="SelectExactlyOne">
          <plugins order="Explicit">
            <plugin name="On (recommended)">
              <description>
Sweep or lift an NPC's feet off the floor with your hand or a weapon and they fall
(ragdoll), like a real person would. Both feet have to leave the floor: lifting one
leg while the other stays planted doesn't trip them.

This works on NPCs leaning on walls, rails or counters too: leaning braces them
against a push, not against a sweep. It is also the part of push/shove most likely
to clash with other physics or animation mods. If NPCs fall when they shouldn't,
turn it off.

PPB.ini key: bFeetLift. Applies within a second, even mid-game.
              </description>
              <conditionFlags>
                <flag name="ppbFeet">On</flag>
              </conditionFlags>
              <typeDescriptor><type name="Recommended"/></typeDescriptor>
            </plugin>
            <plugin name="Off">
              <description>
Lifting or sweeping an NPC's feet never knocks them down. The walk, stumble and
shove knockdown are not affected.

PPB.ini key: bFeetLift.
              </description>
              <conditionFlags>
                <flag name="ppbFeet">Off</flag>
              </conditionFlags>
              <typeDescriptor><type name="Optional"/></typeDescriptor>
            </plugin>
          </plugins>
        </group>

      </optionalFileGroups>
    </installStep>

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Sibling-mod compatibility — ONE option, and it writes a file that
         belongs to another mod, so it is opt-in and says plainly what it
         overwrites.
         PPB deliberately ships NO copy of higgs_vr.ini, activeragdoll.ini or
         NextGenDecapitations.ini. Replacing a user's whole config to change one
         line is not a trade worth making, and the NGD file only ever differed
         by a debug log level.
         ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <installStep name="Compatibility Patch">
      <optionalFileGroups order="Explicit">

        <group name="Dismembering Framework" type="SelectAny">
          <plugins order="Explicit">

            <plugin name="Dismembering Framework - VR freeze fix">
              <description>
INSTALL THIS IF YOU USE DISMEMBERING FRAMEWORK. Skip it otherwise.

Fixes the hard freeze when one NPC dismembers another. That freeze is a thread deadlock:
DF cuts on a worker thread that needs the SKSE task queue, while the main thread holds the
same lock inside the death-frame gear-attach and skinning pass. It predates PPB.

This installs an override for DismemberingFramework.ini that turns DF's threaded hit
processing OFF, which makes the deadlock structurally impossible. PPB then supplies the
death confirmation DF loses that way, by calling DF's own public API on the main thread.
DF keeps all of its own conditions, chances, sounds and limb physics.

The file is stock DF settings with exactly ONE value changed:
    bDeferredHitProcess = 0     (DF default: 1)

OVERWRITES: SKSE/Plugins/DismemberingFramework.ini — if you have tuned DF yourself, copy
that one line into your own file instead of installing this.

If you use DF and do NOT apply this, open PPB_tuning.txt and set dgDeathCut 0, or you may
get doubled cuts.
              </description>
              <files>
                <folder source="10 Dismembering Framework fix" destination="" priority="0"/>
              </files>
              <typeDescriptor><type name="Optional"/></typeDescriptor>
            </plugin>

          </plugins>
        </group>

      </optionalFileGroups>
    </installStep>

  </installSteps>

